```sql
INSERT INTO users (email, plan) VALUES ('a@b.com', 'free');

UPDATE users SET plan = 'pro' WHERE id = 42;

DELETE FROM users WHERE id = 42;

-- upsert: insert, or update if the row already exists (Postgres syntax)
INSERT INTO users (email, plan) VALUES ('a@b.com', 'pro')
ON CONFLICT (email) DO UPDATE SET plan = EXCLUDED.plan;
```

`ON CONFLICT ... DO UPDATE` (Postgres) and `MERGE` (SQL Server, Oracle, and newer Postgres) express "insert this row, but if a conflicting unique key already exists, update it instead" as a single atomic statement — avoiding a separate check-then-insert-or-update round trip that's vulnerable to a race under concurrent writers.
